In this lesson we'll look at perfect squares and how to read their square roots straight off the list. So, a perfect square is just what you get when you multiply a whole number by itself — one, four, nine, sixteen, twenty-five, and so on. A number is a perfect square if it shows up in that list, and here's a handy trick: a perfect square can only end in zero, one, four, five, six, or nine. If a number ends in two, three, seven, or eight, it definitely isn't a perfect square. Next, the square root symbol. If n is a perfect square, the square root of n is the positive number that, multiplied by itself, gives you n. So the square root of forty-nine is seven, because seven times seven is forty-nine. Now spot the pattern. The gaps between consecutive perfect squares go three, five, seven, nine, and so on, growing by two each time, which is a nice fingerprint of squaring. And remember, if the answer isn't a whole number, leave it as a square root, like the square root of fifty. Now let's see it in action.